Title: The Gray Wolf's Ha'nt
Credit: Adapted from the tale by Charles W. Chesnutt (1899)
Order: 6
Production Code: 1x06

FADE IN:

EXT. THE LOW GROUND - NIGHT

AMB: rain on leaves, a swamp breathing

A dark neck of woods by the swamp: tangled undergrowth, standing water, a low mist. Far back among the trunks, something pale moves and is gone.

Uncle Julius McAdoo stands at the edge of the trees under a huge faded cotton umbrella, water streaming off it. He does not step in.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Dey's groun' dat's good fer grapes, en groun' dat's good fer cotton, en groun' dat ain' good fer nuffin 'cep'n' remembrin'.

SFX: a long wailing note, from deep in the wood, that might be wind

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Dat's de wind.

SFX: the note again, closer, and a second one answering it, higher

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Sometimes it's two winds.

MUSIC: STING - a low wolf-howl in the cellos

JULIUS (CONT'D)
I doan ax you ter b'lieve it. I des ax you ter set a minute.

SUPER: "THE CONJURE WOMAN"

INT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - AFTERNOON

A rainy day. The piazza is broad and dry. John has a cigar and a volume of philosophy. Annie, petulant, in a rocking-chair, has been dozing and is bored.

ANNIE
I wish you would talk to me, or read to me--or something. It's awfully dull here today.

JOHN
I'll read to you with pleasure.

He opens the book at his marker.

JOHN (CONT'D)
"The difficulty of dealing with transformations so many-sided as those which all existences have undergone, or are undergoing, is such as to make a complete and deductive interpretation almost hopeless..."

ANNIE
John, I wish you would stop reading that nonsense and see who that is coming up the lane.

John closes the book with a sigh. Up the lane, a huge faded umbrella is making progress toward the house; under it, a pair of trousered legs. It reaches the steps. The old man lowers the umbrella and gets a good dash of rain stepping up.

JOHN (CONT'D)
Why in the world, Julius, didn't you keep the umbrella up until you got under cover?

JULIUS
It's bad luck, suh, ter raise a' umbrella in de house, en w'iles I dunno whuther it's bad luck ter kyar one inter de piazzer er no, I 'lows it's alluz bes' ter be on de safe side. I did n' s'pose you en young missis 'u'd be gwine on yo' dribe ter-day, but bein' ez it's my pa't ter take you ef you does, I 'lowed I 'd repo't fer dooty.

JOHN
I'm glad you came. I should like to consult you. I'm thinking of taking in a piece of new ground. What would it cost to have that neck of woods down by the swamp cleared?

Julius's face turns unusually serious.

JULIUS
I ain' denyin' you could cl'ar up dat trac' er lan' fer a hund'ed er a couple er hund'ed dollahs,--ef you wants ter cl'ar it up. But ef dat 'uz my trac' er lan', I would n' 'sturb it, no, suh. Sho 's you bawn, I would n'.

JOHN
But why not?

JULIUS
It ain' fittin' fer grapes, fer noo groun' nebber is. It ain' no yeathly good fer cotton, 'ca'se it's top low. En it's so nigh de swamp dat de 'coons'll eat up all de cawn.

JOHN
I think I'll risk it.

JULIUS
Well, suh, I wushes you much joy er yo' job. Ef you has bad luck er sickness er trouble er any kin', doan blame _me_. You can't say ole Julius did n' wa'n you.

ANNIE
Warn him of what, Uncle Julius?

JULIUS
Er de bad luck w'at follers folks w'at 'sturbs dat trac' er lan'. Dey is snakes en sco'pions in dem woods. En ef you manages ter 'scape de p'isen animals, you is des boun' ter hab a ha'nt ter settle wid,--ef you doan hab two.

ANNIE
(with growing interest)
Whose haunt?

JULIUS
De gray wolf's ha'nt, some folks calls it. But I knows better.

ANNIE
Tell us about it, Uncle Julius. A story will be a godsend today.

SFX: thunder, distant

Julius lowers himself to the top step, umbrella across his knees.

JULIUS
Way back yander befo' de wah, ole Mars Dugal' McAdoo useter own a nigger name' Dan...

MUSIC: BED IN - low cellos, slow wind

SUPER: "BEFORE THE WAR."

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - QUARTERS - DAY (BEFORE THE WAR)

A big, strong man in the doorway of a cabin, easy and good-natured, wiping his hands. Dan the Wolf. He laughs at something a child says. Across the lane, a few younger men look at him and then away.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dan wuz big en strong en hearty en peaceable en good-nachu'd most er de time, but dange'ous ter aggervate. He alluz done his task, en nebber had no trouble wid de w'ite folks, but woe be unter de nigger w'at 'lowed he c'd fool wid Dan.

A tall, supple young woman crosses the lane with a bucket. Mahaly. She says something to him, sharp and laughing. He answers. The whole quarter turns to watch them.

JULIUS (V.O.) (CONT'D)
Dey wuz a gal on de plantation name' Mahaly. She wuz a monst'us lackly gal,--tall en soopl', wid big eyes, en a small foot, en a lively tongue, en w'en Dan tuk ter gwine wid 'er eve'ybody 'lowed dey wuz well match'.

EXT. THE ROAD - DAY

A stranger on foot, a free man, walks beside Mahaly, talking. She does not look at him. When she reaches the gate he is no farther along than when he started.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De cunjuh man's son tuk ter pester' her. He 'lowed his daddy wuz a cunjuh man, en so he 'd come out all right in de een'.

INT. DAN AND MAHALY'S CABIN - NIGHT

Firelight. Mahaly sits on a stool; Dan, behind her, combs her hair with his fingers.

MAHALY
I wush you 'd do sump'n ter stop dat free nigger man fum follerin' me 'roun'. I doan lack him nohow, en I ain' got no time fer ter was'e wid no man but you.

Dan's hand stops.

EXT. THE ROAD - NIGHT

Dan, alone, steps out of the trees as the free man comes up the road. They stand face to face.

DAN
W'at you mean by hangin' 'roun' my 'oman?

The other man does not answer to Dan's satisfaction. Words become louder. The stranger pulls out a knife. Before it clears his belt, Dan hits him in the head with his fist.

SFX: one blow, a dull sound

The man falls and does not move. Dan stands over him, breathing. He waits. He says something, too low to hear. He walks away.

EXT. THE ROAD - DAWN

A body in the dust. An old man kneels over it: Unk' Jube, old, thin and hard-faced. He carries the dead man away himself, in his arms.

JULIUS (V.O.)
De nex' mawnin' de man wuz foun' dead. Bein' ez it wuz a free nigger, en dey wa'n't no w'ite folks 'speshly int'rusted, dey wa'n't nuffin done 'bout it. En de cunjuh man come en tuk his son en kyared 'im 'way en buried 'im.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT

Dan, with a peck of potatoes, at Aun' Peggy's door. She takes the sack and weighs it in her hand.

AUN' PEGGY
(after hearing him)
Dat cunjuh man is mo' d'n twice't ez ole ez I is, en he kin make monst'us powe'ful goopher. W'at you needs is a life-cha'm. Leabe me a couple er ha'rs fum yo' head, en fetch me a pig ter-morrer night fer ter roas'.

DAN
Yas'm. I'll fetch de pig.

AUN' PEGGY
Den come back ter-morrer fer de cha'm.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT (NEXT NIGHT)

Dan sets a young shote at her feet. Aun' Peggy hands him a small bag of raccoon-skin, stitched shut.

AUN' PEGGY
Put it in a bottle er a tin box, en bury it deep unner de root er a live-oak tree, en ez long ez it stays dere safe en soun', dey ain' no p'isen kin p'isen you, dey ain' no rattlesnake kin bite you, dey ain' no sco'pion kin sting you. Dis yere cunjuh man mought do one thing er 'nudder ter you, but he can't kill you. So you neenter be at all skeered.

EXT. THE RIVER BANK - DAWN

Dan digs under the roots of a live-oak and buries the bottle. He stamps the dirt flat and scatters leaves. A jay-bird, in the branches overhead, cocks its head.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dis yer cunjuh man's jay-bird wuz a monst'us sma't creeter,--fac', de niggers 'lowed he wuz de ole Debbil hisse'f, des settin' roun' waitin' ter kyar dis ole man erway w'en he 'd retch' de een' er his rope.

INT. UNK' JUBE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Jube at the fire. The jay-bird on a perch in front of him, talking in a thin dry voice that we cannot understand. Jube listens, and a slow smile spreads across his face.

EXT. UNK' JUBE'S YARD - NIGHT

A black pot, a hot fire. Jube stirs it with a long stick, murmuring.

SFX: wind, building out of nowhere; a great roar in the pines

MUSIC: STING - wind and brass

EXT. THE RIVER BANK - NIGHT

A storm. The wind tears the live-oak out of the earth and throws it across the bank. Jube stirs another pot; rain comes down in sheets; the bank collapses and carries the bottle into the river. It bobs away, perfectly unconcerned.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - QUARTERS - NIGHT

Dan, sleeping in his cabin, rises, eyes open, and walks out into the night. The old man waits in the lane with a bridle. He puts it over Dan's head. He mounts. Dan, bent under him, runs over rough ground in the dark.

JULIUS (V.O.)
He 'mence' by gwine up ter Dan's cabin eve'y night, en takin' Dan out in his sleep en ridin' 'im roun' de roads en fiel's ober de rough groun'.

INT. DAN AND MAHALY'S CABIN - MORNING

Dan on the bed, grey with exhaustion, as if he had not slept. Mahaly watches him.

EXT. A COUNTRY ROAD - EVENING

Dan walking home. Ahead, at a bend, an old man. Dan's heart jumps, then he remembers the charm, lifts his head, and walks on.

JUBE
Hoddy, Brer Dan? I hopes you er well?

DAN
(wary)
Hoddy, Unk' Jube. I 's p'utty well, I thank you.

JUBE
I's feelin' ez well ez a' ole nigger could feel w'at had los' his only son, en his main 'pen'ence in 'is ole age. But den my son wuz a bad boy, en I could n' 'spec' nuffin e'se. I dunno who killt 'im, en I doan wanter know, fer I 'd be mos' sho' ter fin' out dat my boy had sta'ted de fuss.

He lays a hand on Dan's arm.

JUBE (CONT'D)
Ef I 'd 'a' had a son lack you, Brer Dan, I 'd 'a' be'n a proud nigger. But you ain' lookin' ez well ez you oughter. Dey's sump'n de matter wid you, en w'at's mo', I 'spec' you dunno w'at it is.

Dan's guard is going down, without his knowing it.

DAN
I doan feel well in de mawnin'.

JUBE
Dey is a witch be'n ridin' you right 'long. I kin see de marks er de bridle on yo' mouf. En I 'll des bet yo' back is raw whar she 's be'n beatin' you.

DAN
(wondering)
Yas. So it is.

JUBE
En yo' thighs is des raw whar de spurrers has be'n driv' in you.

DAN
Oh, yas. Dey does hu't pow'ful bad.

JUBE
I knows who it is be'n ridin' you.

DAN
Who is it? Tell me who it is.

JUBE
It's a' ole nigger 'oman down by Rockfish Crick. She had a pet rabbit, en you cotch' 'im one day, en she's been squarin' up wid you eber sence. You better stop her, er e'se you'll be rid ter def in a mont' er so.

DAN
She can't kill me, sho'.

JUBE
She kin make yo' life mighty mis'able. I lacks you monst'us well, Dan. Some er dese days I mought buy you fum yo' marster en 'dop' you fer my son. I'm gwine ter he'p you git rid er dis yer witch fer good en all.

DAN
You is de bes' frien' I got, Unk' Jube. Tell me how.

JUBE
Eve'y night, at ten o'clock, she tu'ns herse'f inter a black cat, en runs down ter yo' cabin en bridles you, en mounts you, en dribes you out th'oo de chimbly. All you got ter do is ter set fer her in de bushes 'side er yo' cabin, en hit her in de head wid a rock er a lighterd-knot w'en she goes pas'.

DAN
How kin I see her in de da'k? En s'posen I hits at her en misses?

JUBE
De bes' plan is ter lemme tu'n you ter some creetur w'at kin see in de da'k, en w'at kin run des ez fas' ez a cat, en w'at kin bite, en bite fer ter kill.

DAN
I'd des ez lief be anything fer a' hour er so, ef I kin kill dat ole witch.

JUBE
Come down ter my cabin at half-past nine o'clock ter-night.

EXT. THE McADOO PLANTATION - ROAD - DUSK

Mahaly, coming home from work. A thin old man steps from the pines, hat in hand, voice honey-smooth.

JUBE
Hoddy do, ma'm. Is yo' name Sis' Mahaly, w'at b'longs ter Mars Dugal' McAdoo?

MAHALY
Yas.

JUBE
Yo' husban' Dan wuz down by my cabin dis ebenin', en he got bit by a spider er sump'n, en his foot is swoll' up so he can't walk. En he ax' me fer ter fin' you en fetch you dere ter he'p 'im home.

She hurries after him. At the cabin door he waves her in first, and shuts it behind them.

INT. UNK' JUBE'S CABIN - NIGHT

A sprinkle of dust. A flash of black fur. A barrel, a board, a rock. A soft furious yowl from under the boards.

SFX: a cat's yowl, inside a barrel

EXT. UNK' JUBE'S CABIN - NIGHT

Half-past nine. Dan in the doorway. Behind him, from inside, the scratching.

DAN
W'at's all dat fuss 'bout?

JUBE
Dat ain' nuffin but my ole gray tomcat. I has ter shet 'im up sometimes.

He gives Dan a new gourd.

JUBE (CONT'D)
Wen you ketches dis witch, take her right by de th'oat en bite her right th'oo de neck. Be sho' yo' teef goes th'oo at de fus' bite. Den come back heah en I'll tu'n you ter yo'se'f ag'in.

Dan drinks, and something goes through him. Down on all fours. A gray wolf, rippling, shaking himself. He is out of the door and gone.

MUSIC: STING - a single howl

EXT. DAN AND MAHALY'S CABIN - NIGHT

The wolf crouches in a bunch of jimson weeds in the yard. A black cat runs up the path toward the door.

SFX: a leap, a snarl, a short cry

He has her by the throat. Blood. And the cat turns back into a woman.

MAHALY
(gasping)
O Dan! O my husban'! Come en he'p me! Come en sabe me fum dis wolf w'at 's killin' me!

The wolf backs away, whining. She calls again; he starts toward her and she cries out louder in terror. He cannot go to her. He hides in the weeds, shaking, with his eyes shut and his teeth clenched.

JULIUS (V.O.)
She did n' knowed dis yer wolf wuz her Dan. En Dan des had ter hide in de weeds, en grit his teef en hol' hisse'f in, 'tel she passed out'n her mis'ry, callin' fer Dan ter de las', en wond'rin' w'y he did n' come en he'p her.

Her voice stops. He stays where he is a long time.

INT. UNK' JUBE'S CABIN - NIGHT

A lighterd-knot flickering on the hearth. The old man nods, asleep, in the corner. The door bangs open and a gray wolf leaps for his throat.

SFX: a struggle, short and ugly

The old man's neck is tough and stringy; Dan holds on until he is sure. He lets go. The old man falls to the floor, rolls his eyes, and smiles.

JUBE
I's eben wid you, Brer Dan, en you er eben wid me. You killt my son en I killt yo' 'oman. En ez I doan want no mo' d'n w'at 's fair 'bout dis thing, ef you 'll retch up wid yo' paw en take down dat go'd hangin' on dat peg ober de chimbly, en take a sip er dat mixtry, it 'll tu'n you back ter a nigger ag'in.

The wolf looks at him with a man's eyes. He climbs on a chair, takes the gourd in his jaws, and drinks.

The old man laughs his last laugh.

JUBE (CONT'D)
Uh huh. I reckon I's square wid you now fer killin' me, too. Dat goopher on you is done fix' en sot now fer good, en all de cunj'in' in de worl' won't nebber take it off.

He gasps once more.

JUBE (CONT'D)
Wolf you is en wolf you stays, all de rest er yo' bawn days.

He dies, grinning.

MUSIC: STING - low, final

INT. UNK' JUBE'S CABIN - LATER

The wolf, frantic, dragging down gourds and bottles from the shelves, trying one after another. Nothing happens. He howls.

EXT. THE FREE SETTLEMENT - NIGHT

The wolf at Aun' Peggy's door. She opens it and looks down. He whines, speaking, in a language she cannot follow. She shakes her head slowly, with something very like grief. She does not know the wolf language.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Aun' Peggy did n' know de wolf langwidge, en couldn't 'a' tuk off dis yuther goopher nohow, eben ef she 'd 'a' unnerstood w'at Dan wuz sayin'.

EXT. McADOO PLANTATION - MORNING

A body in the yard before a cabin. The quarter gathers. Mars Dugal' in shirt-sleeves, bareheaded, bewildered, with white overseers. They examine the throat.

MARS DUGAL'
Dey ain' be'n no wolves 'roun' heah fer ten yeahs er mo'.

The dogs, loosed, follow a scent down to the old conjurer's cabin and find him dead. They follow another scent, and it stops.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Mars Dugal' tuk on a heap 'bout losin' two er his bes' han's in one day. But dat fall de craps wuz monst'us big, so Mars Dugal' say de Lawd had temper' de win' ter de sho'n ram.

EXT. THE LOW GROUND - DUSK

A new grave in the low ground, under the trees. A gray wolf lies on it, with his chin on his paws, not moving. When night comes he lifts his head and howls, and the sound goes on and on.

JULIUS (V.O.)
Dey buried Mahaly down in dat piece er low groun' you er talkin' 'bout cl'arin' up. Ez fer po' Dan, he did n' hab nowhar e'se ter go, so he des stayed 'roun' Mahaly's grabe. En some un 'em said dey seed Mahaly's ha'nt dere, 'bun'ance er times, colloguin' wid dis gray wolf.

A pale woman at the edge of the trees, kneeling, with her hand on the wolf's neck. The wolf turns his head, and leans into it. Then there is only mist.

SFX: wind rising, a long wail, caught in a chimney

INT. THE VINEYARD - FRONT PIAZZA - DUSK (PRESENT)

The air has darkened. The wind whistles at the eaves, slams a loose shutter, and drives the rain into the piazza. A long wailing note comes from the back of the house.

JULIUS
Dat 's des lack po' ole Dan useter howl.

He rises, and takes up his umbrella.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
En w'at I be'n tellin' you is de reason I doan lack ter see dat neck er woods cl'ared up. Co'se it b'longs ter you. But ef you gits rheumatiz er fever en agur, er ef you er snake-bit er p'isen' wid some yarb, er ef a tree falls on you, er a ha'nt runs you en makes you git 'stracted in yo' min', you can't say I neber wa'ned you.

Annie, pale, hand on her throat. John, trying for a jocular word, finds none.

EXT. THE LOW GROUND - DAY

SUPER: "THE FOLLOWING YEAR."

Men with axes and a mule. A neck of woods coming down. John, in shirt-sleeves, walks the cleared ground, looking for a sunken grave or the bleached bones of a wolf. He finds none. Years of leaf-mold and rank growth.

JOHN
(aloud, to nobody)
I cannot say, of course, that some one had not been buried here.

He finds a tree, split open, its trunk hollowed. A bee-tree: a clean opening cut into the side, convenient to a man's arm, and honeycomb inside, clean and recent. Around the opening, the bark is worn smooth by use.

John looks at it for a long time. He looks back toward the vineyard.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - LANE - DAY

Julius, in his coachman's coat, coming up the lane with a covered pail. He sees John waiting on the steps. He slows. He does not break stride.

JOHN
Good afternoon, Julius.

JULIUS
Atternoon, suh.

JOHN
I cleared the low ground.

JULIUS
Yas, suh. I heared.

JOHN
I found no bones.

JULIUS
(gravely)
De bones is mos'ly dus' by now, suh.

JOHN
I found a bee-tree.

Julius's face is a masterpiece of neutrality. The pail in his hand weighs, visibly, more than a pail of water.

JULIUS
Is dat so, suh?

John says nothing. After a moment he steps aside to let the old man pass up the steps. Julius, with great dignity, goes in by the back.

EXT. THE LOW GROUND - NIGHT

The cleared ground, empty, under stars. A faint, thin howl, a long way off. Then it is the wind.

EXT. THE VINEYARD - DUSK

Julius at the log, the pail by his feet, with a spoon. He looks at the lens as he licks it.

JULIUS (TO CAMERA)
Ha'nts is des folks w'at ain' got no place. Dey keeps off de ones w'at ain' got no bizness. Dat's a use.

He tastes the honey, thoughtfully.

JULIUS (CONT'D)
Sho'ly is.

MUSIC: STING - a single howl, in the cellos, resolving into a major chord

FADE OUT.

THE END
